  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDespite the recent thunderstorm activity and cold front passage,\nCanadian wildfire smoke lingers across the region today. As a \nresult, air quality levels have been bouncing between Yellow \nand Orange, spurring the issuance of Air Quality Alerts for \nportions of western Pennsylvania and northern West Virginia \nuntil Midnight tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA powerful storm system tracks from the Pacific Northwest \ntoward the Great Lakes over the next 2 days. By Tuesday, a \ncombination of high energy (1500J/kg to 2500J/kg) and strong \nwind shear (effective shear 35kt-45kts) is expected to create a \nsignificant risk of severe storms across the entire region, \nleading the Storm Prediction Center to issue an Enhanced Risk \n(Level 3 out of 5) for our entire area. Residents should prepare\nfor damaging wind gusts, though tornadoes and large hail are \nalso possible with any discrete supercell storms that are able \nto develop. A Severe T-Storm or Tornado Watch will likely be \nissued during some period of time on Tuesday.\n\nAnomalously high moisture levels with PWATS reaching the 80th \nto 90th percentile raise the risk of flash flooding Tuesday \nevening. Training storms could potentially create localized \nswaths of 2-4 inches of rainfall in a short period of time.\nLocations that saw significant rainfall from this past weekend's\nthunderstorm activity may be especially vulnerable to flash\nflooding due to already saturated soils and increased runoff. \nThe Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has issued a Slight Risk for\nportions of region and a Flash Flood Watch may become necessary\npending further clarity from high-resolution model data over\ncoming days.",
